## Proof

**Answer: 324.**

### Coloring

Color each square of the $19\times 19$ board by the pair $(\text{row mod }2,\ \text{col mod }2)\in\{0,1\}^2$, giving four classes. The class $C=(1,1)$ (odd row, odd column) contains exactly $9\times 9=81$ squares.

### Key lemma

**Lemma.** In any four consecutive configurations, each pawn occupies each of the four color classes exactly once.

*Proof.* Track a single pawn. Suppose its next move is horizontal (the other case is symmetric). Let its color at time $t$ be $(a,b)$. Then:
- $t$: $(a,b)$,
- $t+1$: horizontal move flips the row parity $\Rightarrow (1-a,\,b)$,
- $t+2$: vertical move flips the column parity $\Rightarrow (1-a,\,1-b)$,
- $t+3$: horizontal move $\Rightarrow (a,\,1-b)$,
- $t+4$: vertical move $\Rightarrow (a,b)$.

The four colors $(a,b),(1-a,b),(1-a,1-b),(a,1-b)$ are precisely the four classes, each appearing once. The case where the pawn starts with a vertical move is identical with the roles of the coordinates swapped. Since the pawn must alternate horizontal/vertical moves forever, the claim holds for every block of four consecutive times. $\square$

### Upper bound: $n\le 324$

Consider any four consecutive configurations $C_0,C_1,C_2,C_3$. By the lemma, each of the $n$ pawns is in class $C=(1,1)$ during exactly one of these four times. Hence the total number of pawn-appearances in class $C$ over the four configurations equals $n$.

On the other hand, class $C$ has only $81$ squares, so at any single time at most $81$ pawns can sit in class $C$. Over four configurations this gives at most $4\cdot 81=324$ pawn-appearances. Therefore
$$n\le 324.$$

### Construction achieving $324$

Use only the $18\times 18$ sub-board consisting of rows $1,\dots,18$ and columns $1,\dots,18$. Partition it into $9\times 9=81$ disjoint $2\times 2$ blocks. In each block
$$\begin{matrix}(r,c)&(r+1,c)\\(r,c+1)&(r+1,c+1)\end{matrix}$$
place four pawns and rotate them cyclically around the perimeter
$$(r,c)\to(r+1,c)\to(r+1,c+1)\to(r,c+1)\to(r,c).$$

Each move shifts every pawn one step along its block's cycle:
- $(r,c)\to(r+1,c)$: horizontal,
- $(r+1,c)\to(r+1,c+1)$: vertical,
- $(r+1,c+1)\to(r,c+1)$: horizontal,
- $(r,c+1)\to(r,c)$: vertical.

So every pawn alternates horizontal/vertical moves. All four pawns in a block move simultaneously to the four (vacated) squares of the same block, so no two pawns ever share a square; different blocks are disjoint, so there are no collisions between blocks. The motion is periodic of period $4$ and can be continued indefinitely.

This places $81\cdot 4=324$ pawns.

### Conclusion

The upper bound and the construction match, so the largest number of pawns is

$$\boxed{324}.$$

